WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.100
There are a lot of ways we can break this
movie down, so let’s start with the opening

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:03.600
credits.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.419
They are a montage of mysterious, grungy images
and subliminal messages.

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:11.100
Many of these show preparations made by the
serial killer, John Doe, such as him throwing

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.730
himself into books about the Seven Deadly
Sins, the Christian teachings upon which his

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.820
crimes are based.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.480
Detectives Somerset and Mills eventually use
his library records to track him down.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:23.560
There are also images of razor blades and
John cutting the skin off of his fingers, which

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.100
turns out to be part of the reason that law
enforcement is unable to find any fingerprints

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.880
at any of his crime scenes or even while searching
his apartment.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.730
“We haven’t found a fingerprint yet.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:32.960
Not a single one.”

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:33.700
“You’re right.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.640
I don’t believe you.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.380
Keep looking.”

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.780
The title sequence also includes his darkroom
for developing photos and his writing of personal

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.429
manifestos, both part of his incredible collection
of journals and photographic documentation

00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:48.380
of his work, which he expects to be remembered
and studied for years to come.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.840
“And what I’ve done is going to be puzzled
over, and studied, and followed...

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.180
...forever.”

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.839
I think the same could be said of David Fincher’s
movie, but that’s beside the point.

00:01:01.839 --> 00:01:05.880
A behind-the-scenes documentary shows how
the art department created hand sewn notebooks,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.630
and they speak of wanting to nail the ‘personality’
of Doe’s penmanship.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.930
“They wanted this kind of  ‘obsessive’ quality
to the handwriting.”

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.259
And we see a similar style of writing overlaid
on top of the title sequence.

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:20.200
Some of these messages only appear for as
short as 1/24th of a second, but once deciphered,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.970
we can use them to better understand some
of the hidden details in this film, so stick

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:27.170
around to the end of this video, because I’ll
be unpacking EVERYTHING.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.940
This video is sponsored by Raycon.

00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:37.020
Welcome to Things You Missed, in today’s
episode I’ll be going over 7 things you

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.420
might have missed in David Fincher’s sophomore
film:

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.500
Se-seven-en.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.310
Now you may be angrily typing right now: “Why
aren’t you starting with his first film:

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.310
Alien cubed?”

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.850
Well, I figure I’m gonna do the Alien franchise
at some point, and when I do, I’m gonna

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.850
do them all together.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.109
So before I get to that, there is no shortage
of excellent horror-thriller types in Fincher’s

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:56.780
catalogue, and Se7en is an ideal place to
start.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.899
“You know this isn’t gonna have a happy
ending.”

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.880
Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.320
Some of the messages that briefly flash on
screen during the title sequence look to be

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.640
alphanumeric codes.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.080
For example, D-35 comes up several times and
there’s also a brief D-36.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.920
Another one that comes up a couple times is
C-24.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.000
But what do these strange codes actually mean?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.400
While there are a number of possibilities,
I like to look at the psychological battle

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.250
between the detectives and the killer they
are trying to catch like a game of chess.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.810
In chess, there are a number of strategies
called gambits.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.250
Simply put, a gambit is a calculated risk
taken with the hope that it will give the

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.540
player the upper hand.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.950
So I might allow my opponent to take one of
my pieces in order to ultimately get the positioning

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.720
I want, and get a check-mate.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.360
"If I accept this invitation I'll be giving into his intent.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.820
I can't allow this.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.660
This humiliation!

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:52.820
*gasps*

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.420
One famous instance of this is the Queen’s
Gambit Declined, which is known in the chess

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.880
community as variation D-35.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.660
There’s also a similar variation known as
D-36.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.310
C-24 is another gambit, known as the Bishop’s
Opening.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.120
If you think about it, John Doe’s strategy
to get what he wants from Mills is basically

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:08.760
a gambit.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.340
He willingly gives himself up to the police
in order to set things in motion.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.980
This forces the detectives into the position
he wants them in, because they have to take

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.580
him outside of the city limits to find the
two bodies that he supposedly hid out there.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.670
And ultimately, it leads to a check-mate against
Detective Mills, who can’t restrain himself

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.180
after finding what John Doe did to his wife.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.090
After killing her he delivers her head to
the detectives inside of a box, and by doing

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.300
so, he completes his mission.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.480
She would be the sixth victim, Envy -- and
Mills would take his revenge, making John

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.510
Doe the seventh victim: Wrath.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.190
John Doe set up the pieces perfectly, and
Mills found himself to be nothing more than

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.880
a piece in his masterful game of chess.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.760
“He’s playing games.”

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.760
“No ****.”

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.440
“It’s a part of the game.”

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.820
The experienced Detective Somerset is much
better at not falling for John Doe’s tricks

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:57.880
than Detective Mills, who lets his emotions
overtake him, causing him to make stupid moves.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.380
Perhaps this is because Somerset has some
experience in the game of chess, while Mills

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.540
is more of a basketball guy.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.870
Basketball is also a game that requires strategy,
spacing, and a good gameplan, but basketball

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:11.400
has a much higher pace of play due to it’s
24 second shot clock.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.220
Unlike chess, a lot of moves need to be made
on the fly, which explains why Mills’ actions

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:20.030
on the spot can get him into a lot of trouble,
leaving him as a vulnerable target for a thoughtful,

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:21.700
calculating villain such as John Doe.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.000
Think of Mills like the JR Smith of this movie.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:28.660
"Rebound goes to the Cavs, JR Smith brings it back out!

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:29.780
Throws it to Hill *buzzer*

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:36.320
"It is one of the most brain dead, idiotic things that I've ever witnessed."

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.940
There are many scenes that highlight how Mills’s
impulsiveness contrasts to Somerset’s guile.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.750
Like how Somerset does his studying with long
nights at the library, while Mills skims the

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:45.750
Cliff’s Notes.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:50.240
How Somerset has carefully thought about leaving
his city for 34 years while Mills jumps to

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.730
rent an apartment after five minute tours
with a slimy realtor.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.460
“The soothing, relaxing, vibrating home.”

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.780
*laughter*

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:06.350
How Mills recklessly runs into danger after
a suspect while Somerset asks around for a

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.410
back exit.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:11.190
And ultimately, how Somerset is content with
capturing John Doe, and doesn’t want to

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:15.090
play into his hands any further, while Mills
can’t just let him sit around in prison,

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.280
and is more inclined to accept his offer and
go out to find the remaining two victims.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.700
“Full confession.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.120
I’m in.”

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.840
The differing approaches from the two detectives
are a result of their circumstances, which

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.710
are quite opposite of one another.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:30.760
In both basketball and chess, opponents each
have their own side.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:34.120
Your teammate should be headed in the same
direction that you are, but Mills is celebrating

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.010
his first seven days on the job, while Somerset
is counting down his last seven.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.270
The movie shows us how their experience dictates
how they interact with the city.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.150
One of their first scenes together has them
walking down the street together.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:47.920
The experienced Somerset flows seamlessly
through the pedestrian traffic, while Mills

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.030
is bumping into people and going around people.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.260
He has a lot to learn about his new environment.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.940
“I worked homicide five years.”

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:56.940
“Not here.”

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:57.940
“I understand that.”

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.650
“Well over the next seven days detective,
you’ll do me the favor of remembering that.”

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.990
As they investigate the first crime scene,
the one that represents Gluttony, Somerset

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:09.230
is focused on the job, looking for clues,
but his partner is making small-talk, and

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.610
again, the way the scene is presented says
more than the screenplay itself.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:16.490
The music almost overpowers the volume of
Mills’s frivolous comments, like he’s

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.610
trying to block out the distraction with his
focus.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:22.980
We see Somerset do essentially the same thing
at home, using his metronome to help him sleep

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.900
while horrible things are happening just outside
his walls.

