00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:20.420
Perhaps the biggest and most important difference
between Detective Somerset and Detective Mills

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:24.510
is highlighted in this bar scene, where Somerset
explains why he’s quitting and leaving the

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.500
city.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.880
“I just don't think I can continue to live
in a place that embraces and nurtures apathy

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.770
as if it was virtue.”

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:37.900
He’s trying to warn the younger detective
that in this city, not caring is celebrated,

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.860
and he thinks that attitude is the reason
people in this community get involved with

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.380
drugs, crime, and abuse.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.610
Love, on the other hand, takes effort and
work.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.910
Somerset admits that he’s no better than
anyone else, that he’s part of the problem,

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.430
because he often takes the easy route by being
apathetic.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:56.000
But while he claims to have this cynical view
of himself and his city, his actions suggest

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.000
otherwise.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.860
Early on he suggests to be taken off the case
because he doesn’t want to end his career

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:03.440
on something that will just drag on and on,
but his supervisor, who he’s worked with

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.280
for years doesn’t buy his story.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.660
“I don’t think you’re leaving.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.560
You can’t leave all this.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:12.620
You do this work.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.780
You were made for it and I don’t think you
can deny that.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.640
Maybe I'm wrong."

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.840
Somerset disagrees, however, in that same
scene, he tells off a maintenance man who

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:24.860
is scraping the detective’s name off of
his door.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.340
“Excuse me.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.320
Excuse me.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:30.380
Could you not do that please?”

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.570
It’s almost like a subconscious part of
him doesn’t want his name removed from that

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.540
office, despite his insistence otherwise.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:39.379
“I can’t get involved in this.”

00:08:39.379 --> 00:08:41.180
But moments later, he walks past a banner.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:43.059
“Do you know what you’ve been getting
into?

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:44.250
Are you positive?”

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.640
Maybe he does, maybe he doesn’t.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:49.579
But he does get more and more involved as
the case goes on, and I think he is very much

00:08:49.579 --> 00:08:50.589
aware of it.

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:53.920
At the beginning of the movie, he tells a
cab driver that he’s on his way out...

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:55.200
“Where you headed?”

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.200
“Far away from here.”

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.490
...suggesting that he no longer wants to be
involved with the city, the crime that comes

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.970
with it, and of course, his job bringing those
criminals to justice.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:10.809
Going back to his line on the bar, love costs,
love takes effort and work, but he just doesn’t

00:09:10.809 --> 00:09:12.160
want to put that effort in anymore.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.080
But by the time the fifth murder has taken place,
he’s decided to stay on to the end of the

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:16.620
case.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:20.120
He has no real reason to do that other than
the fact that he cares about the outcome.

00:09:20.129 --> 00:09:23.670
Then, at the end, even after John Doe gets
his way, by putting Mills’ wife’s head

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:27.689
in a box, causing Mills to retaliate and make
John the seventh and final victim… even

00:09:27.689 --> 00:09:32.120
after all of that hurt and suffering, it sounds
like Somerset is still going to stay on.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.000
"Where are you gonna be?"

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:37.240
"Around."

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:44.040
“Ernest Hemingway once wrote, ‘The world
is a fine place and worth fighting for.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.420
I agree with the second part.’”

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.600
One of my favorite movie quotes of all time.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.420
And Se7en also contains one of the better
movie villains of all time, but you probably

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:57.069
won’t see me doing an episode of Horror
History on John Doe, because the whole idea

00:09:57.069 --> 00:09:59.930
behind his character is that he doesn’t
really have a history.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.410
I already discussed the fact he’s basically
removed himself from the crimes he committed

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.250
by cutting the skin off of his fingers.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:09.480
The name John Doe is also kind of ironic for
Somerset and Mills because John Doe is oftentimes

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.559
used as a placeholder name in the world of
law enforcement when a person’s name is

00:10:12.559 --> 00:10:14.149
unknown or needs to be concealed.

00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:17.499
There’s also the fact that for the first
half of the movie, we don’t see John Doe,

00:10:17.499 --> 00:10:21.430
then when we do see him, he’s just a nondescript
guy in a trenchcoat -- great lengths are taken

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:23.120
to preserve his anonymity.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.009
There is actually one exception to this.

00:10:25.009 --> 00:10:29.050
After police discover the third victim, the
one representing Sloth, a supposed media photographer

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:33.490
comes to get some shots, but if we look very closely, it’s actually John Doe,

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.519
returning to the scene of the crime to document
what he had done, probably to add the images

00:10:37.519 --> 00:10:41.709
to his notebooks on top of notebooks about
his work and the relation to the seven deadly

00:10:41.709 --> 00:10:42.260
sins.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:45.880
What he gets instead, is a very pissed off
Detective Mills, who swats at his camera and

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:46.990
chases him away.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:51.009
This gives John Doe the opportunity to rile
him up, to test his restraint, which all plays

00:10:51.009 --> 00:10:54.720
into his plan to make him commit the final
murder to represent Wrath.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.050
This is the reason that he lets him live during
their next encounter, when Mills chases him

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.769
out of his apartment building into an alley,
where John Doe is able to catch him by surprise

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:02.830
and get the upper hand.

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:06.100
John’s objective is to use his crimes to
teach the world of their sins.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:09.699
He had religious reasons behind what he did,
and he felt he had to do something big to

00:11:09.699 --> 00:11:10.860
get the world’s attention.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.230
However, he seemed to believe that he was
doing this entirely for the cause, and not

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.060
at all to bring attention to himself.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:19.339
And that is what made him so difficult to
beat.

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:23.459
Despite being a criminal mastermind, he never
acted like he was anything more than just

00:11:23.459 --> 00:11:25.019
another John Doe.

00:11:25.019 --> 00:11:28.860
One of the images that comes up near the end
of Se-seven-en would bring the movie full

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:29.860
circle.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.610
We started with subliminal messages being
spliced in, and in the penultimate scene,

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.889
an image of Mills’ wife, Tracy appears for
just 1 frame.

00:11:36.889 --> 00:11:40.459
Director David Fincher would bring back the
idea of splicing single frames into movies

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:44.960
for his 1999 film, Fight Club, where one of
the characters in the movie literally does that.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:50.820
"He had one part time job as a projectionist...
because it affords him other interesting opportunities."

00:11:50.829 --> 00:11:53.819
"Like splicing single frames of pornography
into family films."

00:11:53.819 --> 00:11:57.829
But Fight Club, and his previous work, the
1997 action film, The Game don’t really

00:11:57.829 --> 00:11:59.689
fit in any way into the horror-thriller genre.

00:11:59.689 --> 00:12:04.220
For that we have to skip ahead a few years
to 2002 for Panic Room, so lock your doors

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.709
and join me for the next episode of Things You Missed.

00:12:06.709 --> 00:12:10.920
And make sure you subscribe to CZsWorld for
new horrors every week, ring the deathbell

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.120
for notifications and I’ll see you in the
next one.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.970
Assuming we stay inside.

